# Developer Guide — {DB_NAME}

## 1. Roles

| Role | LOGIN | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `{DB_NAME}` | Yes | Owner of every schema/object. The only credential Bytebase uses to run DDL. Treat as a production secret. |
| `{DB_NAME}_readonly` | Yes | Shared credential for BI/reporting tools and read-only human access. |
| `{DB_NAME}_readwrite` | Yes | Shared credential for developers doing DML by hand. Cannot TRUNCATE or DROP anywhere outside `sandbox`. |
| `{DB_NAME}_ops` | Yes | The application/pipeline runtime credential. Cannot TRUNCATE or DROP anywhere outside `sandbox`. |

All four are created `LOGIN NOINHERIT`: `LOGIN` because each connects
directly, `NOINHERIT` so privileges only ever come from the explicit
`GRANT`s below, never silently through role membership.

## 2. Schemas

| Schema | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `bronze` | Raw ingestion — source-aligned, append-mostly. |
| `silver` | Cleaned + validated / conformed data. |
| `gold` | Curated, aggregated serving layer. |
| `audit` | Immutable logs. Read-only for everyone except `_ops`, which may only `INSERT`. |
| `sandbox` | Free-for-all exploration. See §4. |
| `extensions` | Every PostgreSQL extension lives here, never in `public`. |
| `remote` | `postgres_fdw` foreign tables pointing at upstream/remote Postgres servers. |

## 3. Grant matrix

| Schema | `_readonly` | `_readwrite` | `_ops` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `sandbox` | USAGE, CREATE, full DML incl. TRUNCATE, `sandbox.drop_table()` | same | same |
| `extensions` | USAGE, EXECUTE | USAGE, EXECUTE | USAGE, EXECUTE |
| `remote` | USAGE, SELECT | USAGE, SELECT | USAGE, SELECT, INSERT |
| `bronze` | USAGE, SELECT | USAGE, DML (no CREATE) | USAGE, CREATE, DML |
| `silver` | USAGE, SELECT | USAGE, DML | USAGE, DML |
| `gold` | USAGE, SELECT | USAGE, DML | USAGE, SELECT only |
| `audit` | USAGE, SELECT | USAGE, SELECT only | USAGE, SELECT + INSERT only |

`ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES` is set for every schema so **future** tables
automatically pick up the same grants — you never need to re-run a GRANT
statement after adding a table. One nuance worth knowing if you ever extend
this: `DEFAULT PRIVILEGES` in PostgreSQL is scoped to whichever role actually
*creates* the object, not to the schema. In `bronze`, `_ops` has `CREATE`
(pipelines land raw tables directly, outside Bytebase), so the bootstrap SQL
declares `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE {DB_NAME}_ops` in addition to
`FOR ROLE {DB_NAME}` — if you ever grant `CREATE` on another schema to a
non-owner role, you need the matching `FOR ROLE <that role>` default-privilege
statement too, or tables it creates will silently lack grants for everyone
else. This exact gap was caught by testing this template against a live
database, not by inspection — worth re-testing after any change here.

No role other than `{DB_NAME}` ever receives `DROP TABLE`, `TRUNCATE`
(outside `sandbox`), `ALTER TABLE`, `DROP SCHEMA`, or `CREATE SCHEMA`. All
structural DDL against `bronze`/`silver`/`gold`/`audit`/`remote` goes through
Bytebase migrations, run as the owner.

## 4. Why `sandbox` is a deliberate exception

Plain PostgreSQL has no grantable `DROP TABLE` privilege — only the owner (or
a superuser) can drop a table. To let `_readonly`/`_readwrite`/`_ops` behave
like true free-for-all sandbox users — including dropping tables they didn't
create — without widening `DROP` anywhere else in the database, the
bootstrap installs one `SECURITY DEFINER` helper:

```sql
SELECT sandbox.drop_table('my_scratch_table');
```

It's scoped to `sandbox` only (hardcoded schema, regex-validated table name),
owned by `{DB_NAME}`, and `EXECUTE`-granted to all three roles. This is the
one place in the whole database where a non-owner role can destroy data
belonging to someone else — by design, and only here.

`ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES` is set **for every role as a possible creator** in
`sandbox` (not just the owner), so it doesn't matter which of the four roles
creates a table there — everyone else still gets full DML + TRUNCATE on it
automatically.

## 5. Extensions

Installed in `extensions`, all in the bootstrap SQL: `pgcrypto`, `pg_trgm`,
`dblink`, `postgres_fdw`, `uuid-ossp`, `pg_stat_statements`.

`pg_stat_statements` additionally requires, before `CREATE EXTENSION` will
succeed:
1. `shared_preload_libraries = 'pg_stat_statements'` in `postgresql.conf`
2. A full PostgreSQL **restart** (a reload is not enough)

If that hasn't been done yet on a given environment, comment out the
`pg_stat_statements` line in `bootstrap/{BOOTSTRAP_FILE}`, apply the config
change + restart, then re-run the bootstrap (it's idempotent — safe to
re-run in full).

## 6. search_path

```
public, extensions, audit, gold, silver, bronze, sandbox, remote
```

`extensions` sits right after `public` so extension functions
(`gen_random_bytes()`, `uuid_generate_v4()`, etc.) resolve without a schema
prefix anywhere else in the path. `remote` is last on purpose — foreign
tables are meant to be referenced schema-qualified (`remote.some_table`) in
day-to-day queries, to keep it obvious when a query is touching an external
system.

## 7. Day-to-day migration workflow (branch-per-environment)

This repo maps git branches to environments 1:1 — by default `{BRANCH_MAP_SUMMARY}` —
instead of promoting sequentially through every environment on each push to
`main`. There's no single "promote to next stage" pipeline run; promotion
happens by merging one environment's branch into the next one's.

1. Branch off of the branch mapped to your lowest environment (e.g.
   `development`), add a migration under `migrations/`, named with a leading
   version: `202607230001_add_column_x_ddl.sql` (or semver: `1.1.0_x.sql`).
2. Open a PR back into that branch — Jenkins runs `bytebase-action check` on
   every branch/PR and reports SQL Review results.
3. Merge. Jenkins triggers a build for that branch, re-runs the check, then
   rolls out — automatically, if that environment isn't configured to
   require approval (the default for the first/lowest environment).
4. When ready to promote, open a PR merging that branch into the next one
   (e.g. `development` → `main`). This is itself a normal code review. Once
   merged, Jenkins builds the target branch and, if `requireApproval: true`
   for that environment (the default for every environment after the
   first), pauses on a Jenkins `input` step before rolling out.
5. Confirm in Bytebase's Issues view that the release landed in the right
   environment.

See `docs/BYTEBASE_SETUP.md` for the one-time console setup this all
depends on (must be a **Multibranch Pipeline** job, not a plain Pipeline
job, so each branch gets its own build and `env.BRANCH_NAME` resolves), and
the Jenkinsfile itself for the exact branch → environment mapping
(`BRANCH_ENV_MAP`).

## 8. Running this against a managed Postgres (RDS, Cloud SQL, Azure, etc.)

Most managed providers give you an "admin" login that has `CREATEDB` and
`CREATEROLE` but is **not** a true PostgreSQL superuser. Two things in the
bootstrap SQL exist specifically to handle that:

- **Ownership transfers** (`ALTER DATABASE ... OWNER TO`, `ALTER SCHEMA ...
  OWNER TO`, `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE ...`) need the connecting
  role to already be able to `SET ROLE` to the target — PostgreSQL 16
  tightened this so that merely having *created* a role (via `CREATEROLE`)
  is no longer enough on its own. The bootstrap SQL works around this with
  a temporary self-grant (`GRANT {role} TO CURRENT_USER`) right after
  `BEGIN`, and cleans it up again with a matching `REVOKE` right before
  `COMMIT` — you don't need to do anything for this one, it's automatic and
  harmless even if you genuinely are a superuser. Confirmed against a real
  non-superuser CREATEDB/CREATEROLE account while building this template —
  the error to recognize if you ever see it despite this fix is `must be
  able to SET ROLE "..."`, or `permission denied to change default
  privileges`.
- **Three extensions — `dblink`, `postgres_fdw`, `pg_stat_statements` —
  genuinely require actual superuser** (or your provider's equivalent, e.g.
  AWS RDS's `rds_superuser` pseudo-role) to install. No role grant works
  around this; it's a hard privilege check inside PostgreSQL itself. The
  bootstrap SQL wraps each extension individually so a permission failure
  on one prints a `WARNING` and moves on, rather than aborting the whole
  script — you'll see something like:
  ```
  WARNING:  Skipped extension "postgres_fdw" — needs an actual superuser...
  ```
  If you see these: ask whoever has actual superuser (or the provider
  equivalent) to run the one `CREATE EXTENSION` line the warning names,
  then re-run `scripts/run-bootstrap.sh` for that environment — it's
  idempotent, so re-running only fills in what's still missing.

## 9. Anti-patterns this repo avoids

- ❌ Hardcoding passwords in SQL files → bootstrap SQL takes them as `psql -v`
  variables, sourced from `.env`, never committed.
- ❌ Running the bootstrap SQL through Bytebase → it must run manually first,
  since Bytebase can't connect until the owner role it needs already exists.
- ❌ Tables in `public` → always `bronze`/`silver`/`gold`/`sandbox`.
- ❌ Extensions in `public` → always `extensions`.
- ❌ Granting `TRUNCATE`/`DROP` broadly to make `sandbox` work → scoped to a
  single `SECURITY DEFINER` function instead of a blanket privilege.
- ❌ Skipping `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES` → future tables would silently lack
  grants until someone remembers to re-run them by hand.
